            <ArticleTitle>Adverse reaction to metal debris in a painful hemiarthroplasty of the hip.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/jscr/rjw160</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">rjw160</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Adverse reaction to metal debris (ARMD) in total hip arthroplasty surgery is a well-known problem. We present the case of a unipolar hemiarthroplasty requiring revision within 18 months of insertion secondary to an adverse reaction to metal debris. This case demonstrates a rare cause for failure of a hemiarthroplasty following a fragility fracture. We feel that ARMD should be considered in all cases where pain and dysfunction in the presence of any hip prosthesis cannot be explained by routine investigations.</AbstractText>
